- How would you test edge alignment and performance implications?
- Could this approach be generalized to arbitrary widths with minimal changes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests deep knowledge of bit manipulation techniques for combinatorial enumeration without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Next higher number with same popcount (snoob algorithm)
- Handling 64-bit overflow with masking
- Pure bitwise operations and shifts only
- Edge cases are constant-time checks

## Code Example
```javascript
function nextPermutation64(v) {
  const MASK = (1n << 64n) - 1n;
  if (v === 0n || v === MASK) return 0n;
  const c = v & -v;
  const ripple = v + c;
  const ones = v ^ ripple;
  const next = ripple | ((ones >> 2n) / c);
  return next & MASK;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft fixed, branchless bit-twiddling routines across 128‑bit values, a common requirement in high‑performance bitmap or Bloom filter code. It exposes knowledge of SWAR reductions and careful handling of two 64‑bit lanes.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD within a register) reductions
- Handling 128-bit as two 64-bit halves
- Fixed-mask sequences and non-branching sums

## Code Example
```c
unsigned popcount128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t a = x.hi;
  a = a - ((a >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  a = (a & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((a >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  a = (a + (a >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  unsigned hi = (a * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

  uint64_t b = x.lo;
  b = b - ((b >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  b = (b & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((b >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  b = (b + (b >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  unsigned lo = (b * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

  return hi + lo;
}
```

